            <ArticleTitle>Trigeminocervical reflex in fibromyalgia patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1087-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To describe the properties of trigeminocervical reflex in normal subjects and in patients with primary fibromyalgia syndrome (PFS) having neck pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prospective testing of trigeminocervical reflex.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">University hospital electromyography laboratory in Turkey.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Patients with PFS (n=16) and healthy volunteers (n=20).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Trigeminocervical reflex is a brainstem reflex that is evoked by stimulating the sensory branches of the trigeminal nerve and can be recorded from the neck muscles. Electric stimulation of the supraorbital nerve evokes a reflex response (C3) and early reflex response (C1). The mean latencies of C1 and C3 of patients with PFS were compared with normal values.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE" NlmCategory="METHODS">The C1 and C3 latencies of trigeminocervical reflex.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">In healthy volunteers, C3 latency +/- standard deviation was 54.17+/-6.00ms ipsilaterally and 51.25+/-9.26ms contralaterally. The difference was not significant (P=.26). The C1 latency was 17.46+/-4.89ms. In patients with PFS, C1 latency was 13.83+/-4.48ms and the C3 latency was 62.70+/-18.22ms. The difference was not significant between the patients (P=.08) and healthy volunteers (P=.17).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In patients with PFS having neck pain, trigeminocervical connections were not influenced and some other mechanisms may be responsible for pain in these patients.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
